---
// The custom home page (spec F-13.2), one of the two home pages being compared; the other is `/splash/`.
import Editor from '../components/Editor.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import Relations from '../components/Relations.astro';
import '../styles/tokens.css';
import '../styles/editor.css';
import '../styles/home.css';

const GITHUB = 'https://github.com/dxos/dxos/tree/main/packages/devtools/drawing';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>drawing.sh — diagrams your agent can draw</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Install a skill and your coding agent draws architecture diagrams: it writes a few lines of text, drawing lays them out and writes an SVG."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/landing-icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="drawing.sh — diagrams your agent can draw" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Install a skill; your coding agent writes a few lines of text and drawing lays them out into an SVG."
    />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://drawing.sh" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Martian+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Schibsted+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body class="dx-play">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header>
        <a class="mark" href="/">drawing<span>.sh</span></a>
        <div class="top">
          <nav aria-label="Sections">
            <a href="#how">How it works</a>
            <a href="#language">The language</a>
            <a href="#composer">Composer</a>
            <a href="/docs/">Docs</a>
            <a href={GITHUB}>GitHub</a>
          </nav>
          <a class="open-app" href="https://app.drawing.sh/" data-app-link>Open app</a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <main>
        <div class="hero">
          <h1>Diagrams your agent can draw.</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Draw a diagram in your browser, or ask your agent for one. Your agent writes a few lines of text; drawing
            places the boxes, routes every arrow around them and hands back an SVG with zero crossings. Try it below:
            edit the .dx text, or switch to Mermaid to paste a flowchart, which drawing converts to .dx and draws.
          </p>
          <Install />
          <p class="alt">
            Works with Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and any agent that reads <code>SKILL.md</code>. Without an agent:{' '}
            <code>npx -y @dxos/drawing render arch.dx</code>
          </p>
        </div>

        <Editor />

        <section id="how">
          <h2>From a question to a picture in one turn</h2>
          <p class="intro">
            Agents describe systems well and draw them badly. drawing splits the job: the agent says what connects to
            what, and a layout engine decides where everything goes.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>
              <h3>Install the skill</h3>
              <p>One command teaches your agent the diagram language and the <code>drawing</code> command.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ask for a diagram</h3>
              <p>“Draw how a request moves through our services.” The agent reads the code and writes the .dx file.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Get an SVG</h3>
              <p>drawing lays it out in about a second. Put the SVG in a README, a pull request or a design doc.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="language">
          <h2>Say what's related, not where it goes</h2>
          <p class="intro">
            Name the relationship and the right arrowhead follows. There are no coordinates to get wrong; add a
            placement hint only when you want one. The <a href="/docs/language/">language reference</a> has the rest.
          </p>
          <Relations />
        </section>

        <section id="file">
          <div class="file">
            <div>
              <h2>The SVG is the drawing</h2>
              <p class="intro">
                A .dx.svg is an ordinary image, and it also carries everything needed to edit it again. Nothing is
                locked in a service you have to log in to.
              </p>
              <a class="cta" href="https://app.drawing.sh/" data-app-link>Open the app</a>
            </div>
            <ul>
              <li><b>Shows up anywhere</b><span>GitHub, browsers and docs render it like any other SVG.</span></li>
              <li>
                <b>Re-renders from text</b><span
                  >The source rides inside the file, so the next edit is a diff, not a redraw.</span
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                <b>Opens as a live canvas</b><span
                  >Import it into the drawing.sh app, or into a Composer space with <code>dx drawing import</code>, and
                  keep editing it with your team and your agents.</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="composer">
          <div class="file">
            <div>
              <h2>Also in Composer</h2>
              <p class="intro">
                The same diagram editor is a plugin in Composer, DXOS's collaborative local-first workspace. There a
                diagram is one more object in a space, next to documents, tasks and everything else your team keeps, and
                it works with Composer's other plugins and agents.
              </p>
              <a class="cta secondary" href="https://dxos.org/composer/">Request early access</a>
            </div>
            <ul>
              <li><b>Composer is in early access</b><span>Request access to get a Composer space.</span></li>
              <li>
                <b>Start drawing today</b><span
                  >The drawing.sh app is the same canvas, open to everyone now. Export a diagram as a .dx.svg and import
                  it into Composer once you have access.</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>
      </main>

      <footer>
        <span>drawing.sh is built by DXOS. Its diagram editor is also a plugin in Composer.</span>
        <span class="links"><a href="/docs/">Docs</a> <a href="https://dxos.org">dxos.org</a></span>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
